    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class, and the full suite is green. \n\nThe change is complete. Here's what I implemented so that `POST /api/owners` records a `bulkSignupWarning` flag readable back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the field.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `Boolean bulkSignupWarning` field (column `bulk_signup_warning`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it into `OwnerDto` by name automatically.\n\n3. **Schema SQL** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN` column to the `owners` table.\n\n4. **`AssignBulkSignupWarning.java`** \u2014 a new function mirroring `AssignNamesakeCount`: it counts existing owners whose registration date equals this owner's business day and sets the flag `true` when that count is **more than 80**, `false` otherwise. It runs after `BuildOwner` (registration date set) and before `SaveOwner` (excludes the owner being created), so the value is fixed at creation and persisted.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline between `assignNamesakeCount` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\nFor a fresh create (well under 80), the flag persists as `false`, which the acceptance test asserts; the `>80` branch reuses the same daily-accumulation counting path as the existing daily-limit rule (limit 100), so values 81\u201399 produce `true`.",
